## Step 2: Initialise Git Repository and Push to GitHub

After creating the project, I opened the terminal and navigated to the project folder. I then initialised a git repository, staged all files, made the first commit, linked it to a new GitHub repository, and pushed it.

```bash
# Initialise the folder as a git repository
git init

# Stage all files for the first commit
git add .

# Create the first commit with a descriptive message
git commit -m "Initial commit: add RStudio project and example.qmd"

# Link the local repository to a new remote GitHub repository
git remote add origin https://github.com/yourusername/assignment2.git

# Push the commit to GitHub, setting upstream tracking
git push -u origin main
```

**Why:** `git init` creates the hidden `.git` folder that tracks all version history. `git add .` stages every file in the directory. `git commit` saves a snapshot. `git remote add origin` tells git where GitHub is, and `git push -u origin main` uploads the code and sets the default remote branch.

