## Step 3: Create `testbranch`, Modify `example.qmd`, and Push

I created a new branch called `testbranch` to work on changes without affecting `main`. I then modified `example.qmd` by adding a new section, staged and committed the changes, and pushed to GitHub.

```bash
# Create and switch to a new branch called testbranch
git checkout -b testbranch

# (Modify example.qmd here — e.g. add a new section)

# Stage the modified file
git add example.qmd

# Commit the changes with a clear message
git commit -m "Add new section to example.qmd on testbranch"

# Push testbranch to the remote repository
git push -u origin testbranch
```

**Why:** Branches allow us to develop features or make changes in isolation. `git checkout -b testbranch` creates and switches to the new branch in one step. Pushing with `-u origin testbranch` sets the upstream so future `git push` commands on this branch know where to send changes.

