## Step 4: Add Data Folder and Amend the Previous Commit

I created a `data` folder and added the Assignment 1 data file into it. Rather than creating a new commit, I used `git commit --amend` to add the data folder to the **previous commit**, keeping the history clean.

```bash
# Create the data folder and copy in the Assignment 1 data file
mkdir data
cp /path/to/assignment1/data.csv data/

# Stage the new data folder
git add data/

# Amend the previous commit to include the data folder
git commit --amend --no-edit

# Force push because amending rewrites the commit history
git push --force origin testbranch
```

**Why:** `git commit --amend` rewrites the most recent commit to include new changes, avoiding unnecessary extra commits. `--no-edit` keeps the original commit message unchanged. Since amending changes the commit hash, `git push --force` is required to update the remote.

