## Step 10: Add a Plot, Commit, and Undo the Commit

I added a new section with a simple plot to `example.qmd` and committed it. I then used `git reset` to undo the commit while keeping the local file changes.

```{r}
# Simple plot added in this step
plot(pressure, main = "Pressure vs Temperature", col = "steelblue", pch = 16)
```

```bash
# Stage and commit the new plot section
git add example.qmd
git commit -m "Add pressure plot section to example.qmd"

# Undo the commit but keep the changes in the working directory
git reset HEAD~1
```

**Why:** `git reset HEAD~1` moves the branch pointer back by one commit, effectively undoing the commit. Without any flag (defaulting to `--mixed`), the changes remain in the working directory as unstaged edits — the file is not lost, but the commit is removed from history. This is useful when you want to revise a commit before pushing.

To verify the commit was undone but changes preserved:

```bash
# Check that the commit is gone but file changes remain
git status
git log --oneline
```

